\title{Arithmetic Ceilings for Phase Features:\\
Gauss Sums, Near-Orthogonality, and the\\
Impossibility of a Sub-Threshold Lift}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{5 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study the predictive capacity of \emph{root-position phase features} --- the
pairs $\cos(2\pi k r/p)$, $\sin(2\pi k r/p)$ together with the quadratic-residue
indicator $\left(\tfrac{r}{p}\right)$, taken over a range of odd primes $p$ ---
when appended to a baseline regression. An empirical study measured an
out-of-sample $R^2$ lift of $+0.008$ over a baseline of $0.600$ (and $+0.004$
for an extended prime range), with confidence intervals spanning zero, and a
phase-only score of $-0.077$; a pre-registered hypothesis demanded
$R^2 \ge 0.70$. We prove that this outcome is forced. First, we establish a
deterministic ceiling: for any family of $K$ features with pairwise correlations
at most $\delta$ and individual residual correlations at most $\varepsilon$,
every linear combination removes at most $K\varepsilon^2/(1-\delta(K-1))$ of the
residual energy, and the bound is additive over mutually orthogonal blocks.
Second, we evaluate both constants arithmetically: character orthogonality makes
the trigonometric design exactly orthogonal, the Chinese Remainder Theorem makes
distinct prime blocks exactly orthogonal, and the only surviving coupling is a
Gauss sum, giving the normalised value $\delta_p=\sqrt{2/(p-1)}$, which decreases
in $p$. A Gauss-sign dichotomy --- the coupling lives entirely in the sine channel
when $p\equiv3\pmod4$ and entirely in the cosine channel when $p\equiv1\pmod4$,
and equals $\sqrt p$ exactly there --- shows each block has one coupled pair,
sharpening the block constant from $1-2\delta_p$ to $1-\delta_p$, which we show
optimal. The resulting certificate caps the achievable phase-augmented score at
$0.604$, refuting the registered hypothesis before any fit. Third, we prove a
full-frequency degeneracy: over a half-period of frequencies the
quadratic-residue indicator is exactly a linear combination of the phase
features, so it adds no capacity at all. Separately, an exact theory of
transported coefficients shows that a negative out-of-sample score certifies
window locality, and we quantitatively separate the two remaining candidate
explanations of the residual anomaly.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Gauss sums; quadratic residues; additive characters;
near-orthogonal designs; restricted isometry; coefficient of determination;
feature ceilings; window locality.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The empirical setting}

A regression problem attaches to each sample point a target and a feature vector;
a baseline model (a ``footprint dial'') explains a fraction
$R^2_{\text{base}}=0.600$ of the target variance out of sample. The residual
carries visible structure, and an arithmetic hypothesis was registered: that the
structure is explained by the positions $r$ of the underlying objects modulo
small primes, through their phases. Concretely, for each odd prime $p$ in
$\{3,5,7,11,13\}$ (extended in a second arm to all odd primes up to $29$) and a
designated frequency $k$, three real features are attached to a position $r$:
\[
\cos_k(r)=\cos\!\Big(\frac{2\pi k r}{p}\Big),\qquad
\sin_k(r)=\sin\!\Big(\frac{2\pi k r}{p}\Big),\qquad
\QR(r)=\Big(\frac{r}{p}\Big).
\]

The measured outcomes were as follows.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
arm & out-of-sample $R^2$ & lift over baseline\\
\midrule
baseline (footprint dial) & $0.600$ & ---\\
baseline $+$ phases, $p\le 13$ & $0.608$ & $+0.008$ (CI spans $0$)\\
baseline $+$ phases, $p\le 29$ & $0.604$ & $+0.004$ (CI spans $0$)\\
phases only & $-0.077$ & $-0.677$\\
baseline, cross-window & $0.400$ & (in-window $0.600$)\\
registered bar & $0.700$ & refuted\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Two questions arise. Is the near-zero lift a property of \emph{this} fit or of
the feature family? And what does a negative score mean, given that least squares
cannot produce one in sample?

